## Why This Is Asked
 Introduces a new angle: runtime extensibility for serialization formats via a small, testable plugin surface.

## Key Concepts
 - Strategy pattern; - registry-based extension; - runtime pluggability; - minimal surface area.

## Code Example
 ```typescript
 interface Serializer<T> { serialize(value: T): string }
 class JsonSerializer<T> implements Serializer<T> { serialize(v: T){ return JSON.stringify(v) } }
 class XmlSerializer<T> implements Serializer<T> { serialize(v: T){ return `<root>${v}</root>` } }
 class SerializerRegistry<T> { private m = new Map<string, Serializer<T>>(); register(n: string, s: Serializer<T>) { this.m.set(n, s); } get(n: string){ return this.m.get(n)!; } }
 const r = new SerializerRegistry<any>(); r.register('json', new JsonSerializer()); const payload = r.get('json').serialize({a:1});
 ```

## Follow-up Questions
 - How would you handle serializer dependencies or configuration (e.g., encoding options)?
 - How to ensure a misbehaving serializer doesn't crash the whole pipeline?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests ability to design runtime-extensible pluggable components using a registry and strategy-like dispatch, with non-blocking rendering and graceful fallback in a UI/charting context.

## Key Concepts

- Registry pattern
- Strategy pattern
- Async/non-blocking execution
- Runtime extensibility

## Code Example

```typescript
interface Renderer { render(data: any, container: HTMLElement): Promise<void>; }
class RendererRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, new () => Renderer>();
  register(name: string, ctor: new () => Renderer) { this.map.set(name, ctor); }
  get(name: string): Renderer { const ctor = this.map.get(name); return new (ctor!)(); }
}
class Chart {
  constructor(private registry: RendererRegistry) {}
  async render(data: any, container: HTMLElement, preferred?: string) {
    const renderer = this.registry.get(preferred ?? 'svg');
    await renderer.render(data, container);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement graceful fallback if the chosen renderer crashes mid-render?
- How would you expose adding/removing renderers at runtime without hot-reload?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes mastery of runtime extensibility, safe composition of hot-pluggable components, and streaming guarantees in a real-world UI analytics context.

## Key Concepts

- Registry of plugins and factories
- Pipeline composition and deterministic order
- Per-event isolation and context cloning
- Controlled parallelism and backpressure

## Code Example

```javascript
interface Processor {
  process(ctx: any, data: any): Promise<any>;
}
class ProcessorRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, () => Processor>();
  register(name: string, factory: () => Processor) { this.map.set(name, factory); }
  create(name: string): Processor { const f = this.map.get(name); if (!f) throw new Error(`Unknown processor: ${name}`); return f(); }
}
class Pipeline {
  constructor(private steps: string[], private registry: ProcessorRegistry) {}
  async run(ctx: any, data: any) {
    let out = data;
    for (const name of this.steps) {
      const p = this.registry.create(name);
      out = await p.process(ctx, out);
    }
    return out;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you design error handling, retries, and circuit breaking across processors?
- How would you support dynamic reordering without disrupting in-flight events?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a runtime-extendable, observable notification system using plug‑in sinks and safe parallel dispatch.

## Key Concepts

- Runtime registries
- Plugin/Strategy patterns
- Concurrency and backoff
- Idempotency and deduplication
- Observability (metrics, traces)

## Code Example

```javascript
interface Sink { send(message: string, id: string): Promise<void> }
type SinkFactory = () => Sink

class SinkRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, SinkFactory>()
  register(name: string, factory: SinkFactory) { this.map.set(name, factory) }
  create(name: string): Sink | null { const f = this.map.get(name); return f ? f() : null }
}

class NotificationRouter {
  constructor(private registry: SinkRegistry) {}
  async route(routeId: string, message: string, id: string, sinkNames: string[]) {
    const sinks = sinkNames.map(n => this.registry.create(n)).filter(Boolean) as Sink[]
    await Promise.all(sinks.map(s => s.send(message, id)))
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency and duplicate suppression?
- How would you handle sink-specific failures without blocking others?
- How would you instrument with metrics and tracing?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to extend behavior without changing core ingestion logic using a registry/strategy pattern. Evaluates how to design minimal, pluggable interfaces that can be wired via dependency injection and dynamically discovered at runtime.

## Key Concepts

- Registry/Strategy pattern for pluggable parsers
- Stateless parsers and dynamic registration
- Error handling for unknown formats and partial parses
- Thread-safety and DI considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
export interface Parser {
  parse(line: string): any
}

export class JsonlParser implements Parser {
  parse(line: string) { return JSON.parse(line) }
}

export class CsvParser implements Parser {
  parse(line: string) { /* simple split */ return line.split(',') }
}

export class ParserRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, Parser>();
  register(format: string, p: Parser) { this.map.set(format, p) }
  get(format: string): Parser {
    const p = this.map.get(format)
    if (!p) throw new Error(`Unknown format: ${format}`)
    return p
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test unknown formats and ensure graceful degradation?
- How would you handle stateful parsers or formats with schemas?

